    <h1>The Secret Ballot Comes to Maine</h1>
    <p class="dek">How Mainers won the right to vote in private, 1891</p>
    <p class="byline">Published Nov 4, 2026</p>

    <h2 class="section-head">Before We Begin</h2>

    <p class="lede">Most of us take it for granted that we vote in private. For much of American history, we didn&rsquo;t.</p>

    <p>In many places, voters announced their choice aloud, and in Maine it was often a show of hands. Where paper was used, parties printed their own &ldquo;tickets,&rdquo; sometimes in bright colors, so onlookers could tell at a glance how you voted. Employers and anyone offering a bribe could see exactly who you were choosing.</p>

    <p>The private ballot is called the &ldquo;Australian ballot&rdquo; because it began in Australia in the 1850s. It reached the United States only in the late 1880s. Massachusetts passed the first state law in 1888, and by 1900, 38 states had followed. Maine joined in 1891, about 135 years ago.</p>

    <h2 class="section-head">The Story in Eight Pictures</h2>

    <p>Here is how it happened in Maine. The captions are the facts. The characters and their dialogue are illustrative.</p>

